  <!-- Frequently Asked Questions -->
  <div class="bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6 mb-6">
    <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold text-gray-900 mb-5">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
    <div class="space-y-5">
      <div>
        <h3 class="font-semibold text-gray-900 mb-1">How many boxes of laminate flooring do I need for a 500 sq ft room?</h3>
        <p class="text-gray-600 text-sm">For a 500 sq ft room, add 10% waste for standard straight installation (500 × 1.10 = 550 sq ft to buy). If your chosen flooring covers 19.83 sq ft per box, you divide 550 by 19.83 = 27.73, which rounds up to 28 full boxes. If your flooring covers 24.17 sq ft per box, you need 23 boxes.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3 class="font-semibold text-gray-900 mb-1">How many square feet are in a standard box of laminate flooring?</h3>
        <p class="text-gray-600 text-sm">There is no single universal box size. Most residential laminate cartons range between 18 and 25 square feet per box. Common brand standards include Pergo Outlast (18.14 sq ft), LifeProof / Home Decorators (19.83 sq ft), Mohawk RevWood (21.26 sq ft), and TrafficMaster (24.17 sq ft). Always verify the exact carton coverage on the manufacturer box label.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3 class="font-semibold text-gray-900 mb-1">How much waste should I add for laminate flooring?</h3>
        <p class="text-gray-600 text-sm">The National Wood Flooring Association (NWFA) and NALFA recommend adding 10% for standard rectangular rooms installed parallel to the longest wall. Add 15% for diagonal installations, rooms with angled walls, or layouts with numerous door casings and radiator pipes. Add 20% for herringbone or chevron patterns.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3 class="font-semibold text-gray-900 mb-1">Why must I always round UP to the nearest full box?</h3>
        <p class="text-gray-600 text-sm">Flooring manufacturers and home improvement retailers sell laminate exclusively by unopened carton. You cannot purchase partial boxes or loose planks. Furthermore, purchasing all required boxes from the same production run or dye lot prevents noticeable color and sheen variations.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3 class="font-semibold text-gray-900 mb-1">Should I purchase an extra box for attic stock?</h3>
        <p class="text-gray-600 text-sm">Yes. It is strong industry practice to keep at least 1 unopened box of flooring in climate-controlled storage (attic stock). If a plank is damaged by water, deep scratches, or appliance leaks years later, click-lock planks can be individually replaced without replacing the entire floor.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3 class="font-semibold text-gray-900 mb-1">Should I install laminate flooring under kitchen appliances and cabinets?</h3>
        <p class="text-gray-600 text-sm">Install laminate under freestanding appliances like refrigerators and slide-in ranges so they sit level and slide out easily. However, never install floating laminate under heavy permanent kitchen base cabinets or kitchen islands; doing so pins the floor and prevents necessary seasonal thermal expansion, causing buckling and joint separation.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

